.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt KILL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kill ,
.Nm tkill ,
.Nm tgkill
.Nd send a signal to a process or process group
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.Ft int
.Fn kill "pid_t pid" "int sig"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn kill
posts signal
.Fa sig
to the processes selected by
.Fa pid .
Signals 1 through 64 are accepted.
Signal 0 checks existence and permission without posting a signal.
.Bl -tag -width "pid < -1"
.It Fa pid No > 0
Select the process with this process ID.
.It Fa pid No = 0
Select the caller's process group.
.It Fa pid No < -1
Select the process group whose ID is
.No - Ns Fa pid .
.It Fa pid No = -1
Broadcast to all processes is not implemented and returns
.Er ESRCH .
.El
.Pp
Zombie processes are not targets.
A group request posts the signal to every member the caller may signal.
It succeeds if at least one member is permitted, returns
.Er EPERM
if members exist but none is permitted, and returns
.Er ESRCH
if no live member exists.
.Pp
A caller with effective user ID 0 may signal any target.
Otherwise, either the caller's real or effective user ID must match the
target's real or saved user ID.
For
.Dv SIGCONT
only, membership in the target's session also permits the call.
This session exception does not apply to signal 0.
.Ss Thread signal calls
Rach2 has no threads.
The kernel calls
.Nm tkill
and
.Nm tgkill
address a process by its process ID.
.Nm tkill
takes a positive thread ID and a signal.
.Nm tgkill
takes a positive thread group ID, a positive thread ID, and a signal;
both IDs must identify the same process.
Zero and negative IDs are errors in these calls, never group selectors.
Their permission and signal rules are those of
.Fn kill .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 37 for
.Nm kill ,
238 for
.Nm tkill ,
and 270 for
.Nm tgkill .
For
.Nm tkill ,
EBX is the thread ID and ECX the signal.
For
.Nm tgkill ,
EBX is the thread group ID, ECX the thread ID, and EDX the signal.
Musl declares neither function.
Its
.Fn raise
uses
.Nm tkill
to signal the caller; a direct
.Fn syscall
is needed to issue
.Nm tgkill .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn kill
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return 0 on success or the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
places a child in its own process group, sends
.Dv SIGTERM
with
.Ql "kill(-cpid, SIGTERM)" ,
and checks that the child's wait status reports that signal.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The signal is outside 0 through 64, or a thread ID or thread group ID
given to
.Nm tkill
or
.Nm tgkill
is not positive.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the target, or may not signal any live member
of the selected group.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No selected live process exists, the selected group has no live members,
or
.Nm tgkill
was given IDs that do not identify the same process.
.Fn kill
also returns this error for
.Fa pid
-1 or the most negative 32-bit integer, and for
.Fa pid
0 when there is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getuid 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
